{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011F7VfiFGFTFERHZZZdemhT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# pwm\n2\t\n3\tAn 8-bit pulse-width-modulation generator with a period of 256 clock cycles.\n4\t\n5\t## Ports\n6\t| Port    | Dir | Width | Description |\n7\t|---------|-----|-------|-------------|\n8\t| clk     | in  | 1     | Rising-edge clock |\n9\t| rst_n   | in  | 1     | Asynchronous active-low reset |\n10\t| duty    | in  | 8     | Requested duty cycle (number of high cycles per 256-cycle period) |\n11\t| pwm_out | out | 1     | PWM output (combinational from registers) |\n12\t\n13\t## Behavior\n14\t- Internal registers: a free-running 8-bit counter `cnt` and an 8-bit latched duty value `duty_q`.\n15\t- While `rst_n` is low, `cnt = 0` and `duty_q = 0`.\n16\t- On every rising edge of `clk`, `cnt` increments by 1, wrapping 255 -> 0.\n17\t- `duty_q` only changes at the period boundary: on the rising edge at which `cnt` goes from\n18\t  255 to 0, `duty_q` takes the value of `duty` sampled at that edge. At every other edge `duty_q`\n19\t  holds. Changes to `duty` in the middle of a period therefore have no effect until the next\n20\t  period begins, and the new value applies starting from the cycle in which `cnt == 0`.\n21\t- `pwm_out = (cnt < duty_q)`. So in each period the output is high for exactly `duty_q`\n22\t  cycles (cnt = 0 .. duty_q-1) and low for the remaining `256 - duty_q` cycles. `duty_q = 0`\n23\t  means the output is low for the whole period; `duty_q = 255` means high for 255 of 256 cycles.\n24\t- Because `duty_q` resets to 0, the output is low for the entire first period after reset\n25\t  (cnt = 0..255), regardless of `duty`.\n26\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9e06281c-4a6c-43ad-bc52-75887ad4d880","uuid":"cc4248fd-0a9f-4e8e-9c4a-81eb7d677a42","timestamp":"2026-10-19T23:43:10.964Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\abhig\\Documents\\New folder\\assay\\runs\\v1__pwm__t3\\workspace\\spec.md","content":"# pwm\n\nAn 8-bit pulse-width-modulation generator with a period of 256 clock cycles.\n\n## Ports\n| Port    | Dir | Width | Description |\n|---------|-----|-------|-------------|\n| clk     | in  | 1     | Rising-edge clock |\n| rst_n   | in  | 1     | Asynchronous active-low reset |\n| duty    | in  | 8     | Requested duty cycle (number of high cycles per 256-cycle period) |\n| pwm_out | out | 1     | PWM output (combinational from registers) |\n\n## Behavior\n- Internal registers: a free-running 8-bit counter `cnt` and an 8-bit latched duty value `duty_q`.\n- While `rst_n` is low, `cnt = 0` and `duty_q = 0`.\n- On every rising edge of `clk`, `cnt` increments by 1, wrapping 255 -> 0.\n- `duty_q` only changes at the period boundary: on the rising edge at which `cnt` goes from\n  255 to 0, `duty_q` takes the value of `duty` sampled at that edge. At every other edge `duty_q`\n  holds. Changes to `duty` in the middle of a period therefore have no effect until the next\n  period begins, and the new value applies starting from the cycle in which `cnt == 0`.\n- `pwm_out = (cnt < duty_q)`. So in each period the output is high for exactly `duty_q`\n  cycles (cnt = 0 .. duty_q-1) and low for the remaining `256 - duty_q` cycles. `duty_q = 0`\n  means the output is low for the whole period; `duty_q = 255` means high for 255 of 256 cycles.\n- Because `duty_q` resets to 0, the output is low for the entire first period after reset\n  (cnt = 0..255), regardless of `duty`.\n","numLines":26,"startLine":1,"totalLines":26}}}
